---
layout: article
title: Captions
description: Add WebVTT or SubRip captions to an Appwrite video, use subtitle tracks embedded in the source, and set a default language for players.
---

**Captions** are text tracks attached to a [video](/docs/products/videos/videos). Players pick them up from the master manifest and let viewers switch languages. A caption comes from a file in Storage, from the caption editor in the Console, or from the source file itself. Appwrite does not transcribe speech, so to caption a video that has no track yet, upload the output of a transcription service or write the track in the caption editor.

Captions make a video usable with the sound off, accessible to deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers, and watchable in more languages. Each language is its own track, so one video can carry English, Spanish, and Japanese captions side by side.

# Caption files {% #caption-files %}

A caption file is a list of **cues**: a start time, an end time, and the text to show in between. Appwrite accepts the two most common formats.

**WebVTT** (`.vtt`) is the web standard:

```text
WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Welcome to the launch.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.000
Today we are shipping three things.
```

**SubRip** (`.srt`) numbers each cue and uses a comma before milliseconds:

```text
1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome to the launch.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,000
Today we are shipping three things.
```

Most editing tools and transcription services export one of these. Save files as UTF-8 so accents and non-Latin scripts display correctly.

# Uploaded captions {% #uploaded-captions %}

Upload a **WebVTT** (`.vtt`) or **SubRip** (`.srt`) file to a Storage bucket, then attach it to the video. Appwrite converts it to WebVTT and segments it for streaming in the background. The bucket must allow these extensions. Any other file returns the `video_caption_not_valid` error.

1. Open the video and select **Captions**.
2. Click **Upload caption**.
3. Select a **Subtitle file** from Storage.
4. Choose a **Language**. The **Name** fills in from it, for example `English`, and you can change it.
5. Turn on **Default caption** to have players show it automatically.
6. Click **Create**.

{% only_dark %}
![Captions tab with an English caption marked as the default track](/images/docs/videos/dark/captions.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Captions tab with an English caption marked as the default track](/images/docs/videos/captions.avif)
{% /only_light %}

With a server SDK:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const videos = new sdk.Videos(client);

const caption = await videos.createCaption({
    videoId: '<VIDEO_ID>',
    bucketId: '<BUCKET_ID>',
    fileId: '<CAPTION_FILE_ID>',
    name: 'English',
    code: 'eng',
    xdefault: true // optional
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.videos import Videos

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

videos = Videos(client)

caption = videos.create_caption(
    video_id = '<VIDEO_ID>',
    bucket_id = '<BUCKET_ID>',
    file_id = '<CAPTION_FILE_ID>',
    name = 'English',
    code = 'eng',
    default = True # optional
)
```

